00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:56.680
David Bovill: I would say, but for instance, so let's just talk about Hitchhikers and what that kind of story and brand means. So first of all.

307
00:50:58.890 --> 00:51:07.459
David Bovill: There's a deep dive into what a hitchhiker is. A hitchhiker is this kind of 1970s idea of thumbing a lift.

308
00:51:07.670 --> 00:51:12.020
David Bovill: It's also the characters from the movie and the book. Okay, fair enough.

309
00:51:12.130 --> 00:51:13.609
David Bovill: But it's also about…

310
00:51:14.390 --> 00:51:23.909
David Bovill: These two concepts of hiking, which is adventurously going out into the wilderness, and also hitching.

311
00:51:23.980 --> 00:51:40.929
David Bovill: tagging along, joining someone else, supporting someone else's journey, and being open to wherever that journey takes you. And so we're particularly looking at, rewilding the concept of

312
00:51:41.190 --> 00:51:48.790
David Bovill: Any form of nomads, so you've got from

313
00:51:49.000 --> 00:52:03.690
David Bovill: refugees, asylum seekers, diaspora, hitchhikers, climate refugees, to digital nomads. And these are also people who move between one knowledge community to another, bridging these communities.

314
00:52:03.900 --> 00:52:20.170
David Bovill: We also use the Babelfish in terms of translating the idioms, the language, and the knowledge from one community to another. So, like, from a legal tech community to a group of young people who like manga in Japan.

315
00:52:20.280 --> 00:52:25.930
David Bovill: And there are lots of AI characters in Hitchhiker. There's obviously Marvin, who's a…

316
00:52:25.960 --> 00:52:41.810
David Bovill: gloriously cynical and skeptical AI. You've got genuine people personality, so we can very much place the current debate about the people who hate AI and the people who are big fans of AI, how we govern them, how we make them private.

317
00:52:41.810 --> 00:52:46.439
David Bovill: and engage a community in that debate using these fictional characters.

318
00:52:46.940 --> 00:52:59.780
David Bovill: our governance of the mutual is based basically on citizen assembly methodology. Our joke is that, for instance, the answer to life in the universe and everything is 42, which is the minimal viable size of a

319
00:52:59.780 --> 00:53:08.020
David Bovill: of a statistically valid citizen assembly. They basically raised between 40 and 300 as a classic assembly.

320
00:53:08.390 --> 00:53:15.439
David Bovill: So we've got lots of the memes and the characters from Hitchhikers in the storyline.

321
00:53:15.460 --> 00:53:32.350
David Bovill: And things we will provoke and engage communities with that kind of playful humor that also avoids some of the hard political fight, you know, between left and right or pro-AI and anti-AI. We can playfully imagine a future together.

322
00:53:33.260 --> 00:53:46.239
David Bovill: do workshops about that and publish guides together with multiple perspectives. So I can talk a little bit about that. But you also, Grace, you asked about what that kind of onboarding experience is.

323
00:53:47.260 --> 00:53:56.359
David Bovill: There are many facets to it, but I give you an example of the workshop that we did internally, and how we expect to take that

324
00:53:56.520 --> 00:54:04.799
David Bovill: In the events that we do. So, we had the artists discuss, first of all, what they

325
00:54:05.120 --> 00:54:11.609
David Bovill: It's based on a particular community project methodology called Project Cycle Management, but I call it,

326
00:54:11.850 --> 00:54:25.370
David Bovill: moaning and a dragon workshop. It's where you get people to 1st of all voice what they really hate. In our case it was about the the art economy and the art market, and we

327
00:54:25.480 --> 00:54:33.090
David Bovill: Do workshops where we, arrange them in causal loop diagram type way on the wall.

328
00:54:33.310 --> 00:54:52.879
David Bovill: and then we flipped them into positive objectives. And then the second day we did basically a dragon dreaming workshop. And in those workshops we got the the artist because we could say that this information was being privately stored on these raspberry pies. It wasn't online. It wasn't going anywhere else. And we had some very

329
00:54:53.530 --> 00:54:55.370
David Bovill: politically conscious…

330
00:54:55.710 --> 00:55:11.139
David Bovill: members of the workshop who would never have done that workshop if we were recording it on Zoom in any other way. And the kind of onboarding involved people revealing a lot about their personal experience.

331
00:55:11.140 --> 00:55:25.360
David Bovill: what their income was like, what their needs were, what they could offer people, including things like mental health issues. We had two of the people who were on antidepressants and discussing that. And all that kind of…

332
00:55:25.400 --> 00:55:41.509
David Bovill: Very intimate conversation at the group level, enables us to create better and more powerful group projects, but that's only possible if we can really strongly, guarantee the confidentiality and the trust.

333
00:55:41.820 --> 00:55:56.210
David Bovill: of that community, and then, at the same time, still enable the networking in terms of the matching of needs and offers between those communities. So, that's one example.

334
00:55:56.270 --> 00:56:03.760
David Bovill: It's similarly if you start to run citizen assemblies in particular.

335
00:56:03.930 --> 00:56:23.619
David Bovill: precarious groups. Those assemblies want to discuss things internally, being assured that, first of all, their voice will be listened globally, or in the governance context, but also that the details of those discussions are protected. So, all of these use cases

336
00:56:23.620 --> 00:56:34.870
David Bovill: We're actively engaged in with hitchhikers and we require this citizens assembly.

337
00:56:34.930 --> 00:56:46.949
David Bovill: diversity of stakeholders in order to deliberate the big topics of the time in Hitchhikers to produce these guides, so there's an active kind of playfulness to seek out

